[claude] 🔧 mcp__kernelgen__finalize_round: {"conclusion": {"round_num": 1, "expectation_status": "baseline", "root_cause": "No prior measurement in this workspace; baseline established. The 0.3456 geo_mean splits into two clearly different regimes. (a) Large geometry (4x32x128x128 -> 256x256, 8.4M outputs) takes 0.63 ms against a 0.21-0.27 ms reference; moving ~21 MB in 0.63 ms is ~33 GB/s, so the kernel is addressing/DMA bound, not bandwidth or compute bound. (b) Small geometry (1x16x64x64 -> 128x128, 0.26M outputs) takes 0.103 ms while the reference full call is only 0.031-0.038 ms even though it launches 5 device tasks, so ~0.08 ms of our 0.10 ms is host launch path, not device work.", "perf_gap_analysis": "Baseline round, so there is no previous round to compare against. The frozen expectation was that the seed reproduces roughly 0.34x with per-workload latencies near 0.63 ms (large) and 0.11 ms (small); the measured values are 0.6296/0.6309/0.6266 ms (timing-1/3/5) and 0.104/0.103/0.102 ms (timing-0/2/4), confirming both the level and the two-regime shape of the cost. geo_mean 0.3456 vs the inherited 0.3395 is within run-to-run noise.", "next_suggestion": "Attack the large-geometry device path first, since it is ~30x above the pure I/O floor and a 5x gain there alone would move geo_mean near 0.8. The seed's fast kernel (i) always applies element masks even when BR divides n_rows and IW is a power of two, and (ii) stores a (BR, IWP, 2) 3-D tile whose only contiguous axis has length 2. Test unmasked loads/stores plus an explicit reshape/interleave to a 2-D (BR, 2*IWP) contiguous store, and larger BR, in one bounded debug probe before committing a round.", "optimization_level": "L2_memory", "knowledge_assessments": []}}
