{
  "status": "PASSED",
  "summary": "Best measured round (round 1, PASSED 24/24, geo_mean 0.3456x) is the inherited seed architecture: a 2-D fast path with aligned row loads plus on-chip tl.gather for the horizontal taps, and a flat 1-D general path, measuring 0.104/0.103/0.102 ms on the 1x16x64x64 geometries (0.30-0.37x) and 0.630/0.631/0.627 ms on the 4x32x128x128 geometries (0.33-0.43x). Bounded target probes established the key lessons for the unmeasured redesign: flat 1-D contiguous blocks move the same volume ~6x faster per element than 2-D tiles with computed row bases, a flat load one element off costs only ~3% while a 2-D tile with a one-element minor-axis offset costs 2.2x, tl.gather on a flat vector scalarises at ~10 us per program, and per-program fixed cost dominates below ~4-8 K elements per program while UB caps the fast path at ~2 K elements per program. The resulting all-flat 2x fast path (nine shifted flat loads instead of gathers) was written and its general path verified 24/24 numerically via a debug job, but its fast path could never be measured: the run was cancelled by the operator budget after the evaluator's preflight began failing intermittently in its own empty_cache() call, before any candidate code ran."
}
```
